{
 "artifact_type": "omp_ninfer_measurement",
 "schema_version": 1,
 "experiment": "EXP-061",
 "title": "RTX 5090 keep-warm load: a single-warp kernel at 30% duty holds P1 at about 90 W, and a grace period brings new sessions after idle back to 0.17-0.20 s TTFT",
 "generated_utc": "2026-09-26T12:18:13Z",
 "status": "passed",
 "question": "What is the lightest GPU load that keeps the RTX 5090 out of its slow idle P-states between agent requests, what does it cost in power, and does running it only while the engine is idle bring new sessions after idle back to the back-to-back latency EXP-060 measured?",
 "answer": "A single-warp kernel spinning 30 ms of every 100 ms (30% GPU utilization) holds P1 with the memory clock at 13,801 MHz; 25% steps down to P8 after 13.7 s, and the same kernel at 1-20% reaches P8 within 3.4-7.4 s. Neither all-SM spins at 10-20% nor device-to-device copies of 64-256 MiB every 20-100 ms hold the card. Beside production's binary served without Nsight Systems, the 30% load drew 88.8-91.6 W against 29.0 W idle in P8 (87.3 W alone; 40% draws 117-118 W). Run as a grace period, resumed as each response returned and paused 0.1 s before the next request, it held P1 through 12 s and 60 s gaps: new sessions prefilled in 0.155 s and 0.180 s (TTFT 0.173 s and 0.201 s) against 0.155-0.159 s back to back and 0.353 s (TTFT 0.417 s) for the same session after a 30 s gap without it. The control gap confirms EXP-060's step schedule without Nsight Systems: P3 at 1.9 s, P5 at 6.7 s and P8 at 8.5 s after the last response.",
 "decision": "Open, measure only: nothing built or shipped; production stays on v0.8.1 with every host setting unchanged. The lever keeps output bytes. Its engineering form is an engine-side grace period: after a request completes, ninfer-serve launches a single-warp spin kernel for 30-35 ms of every 100 ms on its own stream until the grace period ends or a request is admitted. Admission stops it immediately (a side process would contend for the GPU while requests run). Cost: about 60 W above idle while held, so about 1 Wh per minute of grace. Benefit: new-session TTFT about 0.2 s instead of about 0.4 s; continuations and cold starts that arrive after idle should gain too (not measured). 138 of 446 agent requests in production's v0.7.0 traffic arrived 5 s or more after the previous one.",
 "method": {
  "keepwarm": "keepwarm.cu (scratch): a schedule of segments, each a full-GPU burst, idle, or a periodic unit of work launched and synchronized at the start of every period: a single-warp kernel spinning a given wall time (%globaltimer), an all-SM spin, or a device-to-device copy. SIGUSR1 pauses the periodic work and SIGUSR2 resumes it.",
  "sweeps": "2026-09-26 11:46-11:51Z and 11:54-12:01Z, RTX 5090: production held and stopped, keepwarm alone in a CUDA 13.1.2 base container, no inference server; every load segment ran 30 s after a 3 s full-GPU burst, so it started from P1 as the card does when a request ends; nvidia-smi sampled P-state, SM and memory clocks, power, utilization and temperature every 50 ms. A load holds if no P5 or P8 sample appears during its segment; power and clocks are medians over its last 10 s.",
  "grace_windows": "2026-09-26 12:03-12:07Z (production's image and binary 5b2f2471 under Nsight Systems, pause 0.3 s before each request) and 12:12-12:16Z (without Nsight Systems, pause 0.1 s before each request, the 30 s gap left without the load as a control), each with production's arguments on a side port, production held and restored healthy: one cold request whose ~30K-token system message and 12 tool definitions form the stable prefix, then six new two-message sessions after 0, 12, 0, 60, 0 and 30 s; the client resumed the 30% load as each response returned before a nonzero gap."
 },
